# Domain boundaries rule

When writing or modifying production code, always protect the domain model from UI, persistence, and serialization leakage.

## 1. Main objective

The domain model must represent the business meaning of the application, not the technical shape of:
- JavaFX controls
- protobuf messages
- JAXB/XML classes
- ZIP archive entries
- file chooser concerns
- desktop UI state

Do not let technical infrastructure become the domain model.

## 2. Domain purity

Domain classes must stay free from dependencies on:
- JavaFX UI classes
- TestFX
- protobuf-generated classes
- JAXB annotations and JAXB runtime types for current persistence logic
- ZIP/archive-specific APIs as part of the domain model
- application dialog services
- file chooser abstractions

The domain layer may use standard Java types and carefully chosen shared abstractions, but it must not be shaped around framework details.

## 3. No protobuf leakage into domain

Generated protobuf classes are serialization-layer artifacts.

Do not:
- store protobuf messages inside domain objects
- expose protobuf message types in domain APIs
- return protobuf-generated types from domain methods
- pass protobuf message builders through the application/domain layers

Instead:
- map protobuf messages to domain objects explicitly
- keep protobuf confined to serialization/persistence code

## 4. No JAXB leakage into domain

Legacy XML compatibility exists only for reading old archives.

Do not:
- annotate domain classes for legacy JAXB persistence unless the codebase has an unavoidable transition state
- treat JAXB-bound classes as the domain model
- allow new domain APIs to depend on legacy XML concerns

If legacy XML compatibility needs dedicated classes, isolate them clearly.

## 5. No JavaFX leakage into domain

Do not let domain objects depend on:
- `Node`
- `Stage`
- `Scene`
- `ObservableList`
- JavaFX properties as the default domain representation
- UI selection or focus state
- control-specific formatting state

If the UI needs observable wrappers or presentation-specific state, place that in a view model or presentation model layer.

## 6. Domain invariants belong in domain code

Business rules and domain invariants should live in domain or dedicated domain services, not in:
- controllers
- protobuf mappers
- archive readers
- XML compatibility loaders
- random utility classes

Do not bury business meaning inside technical conversion code.

## 7. Mapping boundaries must be explicit

Any crossing between:
- domain <-> protobuf
- domain <-> legacy XML compatibility model
- domain <-> UI form/presentation model
- domain <-> persistence DTO

must be explicit and testable.

Do not silently merge layers by sharing the same mutable structure everywhere.

## 8. Persistence model is not domain model

A persisted descriptor structure may resemble the domain, but it is not automatically the same thing.

Treat persisted structures as:
- serialization schema objects
- persistence DTOs
- compatibility DTOs

Map them explicitly into domain objects that express business meaning.

## 9. UI state is not domain state

Do not mix into domain classes:
- currently selected node
- expanded/collapsed tree state
- panel visibility
- unsaved-changes badges
- drag preview coordinates
- temporary input validation markers

Those belong to UI/presentation logic, not to the core diagram model unless truly part of the business meaning.

## 10. Archive concerns are not domain concerns

Do not embed archive entry names, ZIP handling rules, descriptor precedence rules, or format metadata logic directly into domain classes.

The domain should not know whether it came from:
- `descriptor.pb`
- `save.xml`
- a ZIP archive
- a future descriptor format

Those are persistence concerns.

## 11. Avoid anemic but also avoid technical pollution

The domain should not be a dumb bag of getters/setters if meaningful invariants and operations exist.
At the same time, do not compensate by filling domain classes with technical persistence/UI concerns.

Prefer:
- meaningful domain behavior
- explicit invariants
- clean domain-oriented methods

Avoid:
- technical helper clutter in domain objects
- serialization-specific convenience methods in domain classes
- UI formatting logic in domain methods

## 12. Naming must reflect domain meaning

Use names that express domain concepts, not infrastructure leakage.

Prefer:
- `Diagram`
- `PatternElement`
- `ThreadPath`
- `DiagramMetadata`

Avoid names in domain code such as:
- `ProtoDiagram`
- `XmlDiagram`
- `ZipDiagram`
unless they explicitly belong to non-domain layers.

## 13. Changes in technical format must not force domain churn

A change in persistence format, versioning, archive structure, or UI technology should not require broad invasive changes in the domain model.

If changing protobuf schema or archive structure requires changing many domain classes, the boundary is probably weak and should be improved.

## 14. Tests should reinforce boundaries

When touching domain-related code, prefer tests that:
- verify domain invariants and behavior directly
- verify mapper correctness separately
- avoid forcing domain tests to go through JavaFX or archive infrastructure unnecessarily

Do not normalize bad boundaries by only testing everything end-to-end.

## 15. Mandatory cleanup when touching weak boundaries

When modifying an area where:
- protobuf types leak into application/domain code
- JAXB compatibility code leaks into normal domain flow
- JavaFX properties leak into domain
- archive entry logic leaks into domain classes

improve the boundary if reasonably possible.

Do not add new code that deepens the leakage.

## 16. Output expectations

Generated or modified code must:
- keep the domain model technically clean
- keep protobuf/JAXB/JavaFX/archive concerns outside the domain
- preserve explicit mapping boundaries
- improve long-term maintainability

If the existing codebase contains weaker patterns, improve the touched area toward this standard instead of copying them.